WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:15.420
The next item on the agenda is the New Care
Models Programme. Ian, I am going to invite

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.950
you to introduce it, but also to introduce
Karen Baker, who is leading the Isle of Wight

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.080
Vanguard, who will also make a presentation
to us. Karen, thank you for coming here today

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.440
and for joining us; we look forward to what
you have to say.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:41.060
Ian Dodge Thank you, Chair. I will draw out
six points from the paper. First, the energy

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:49.950
and enthusiasm in the local sites is palpable
on the visits, as is the strength of the relationships

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:57.460
between the constituent partners, and I think
that gives us good grounds for optimism for

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.149
the eventual success of the Vanguard sites.

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:13.700
Secondly, our job nationally is to help solve
problems for and with local sites, and to

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:22.570
help them seize the opportunities ahead of
them. In July we intend, as set out in our

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:30.590
Business Plan, to publish the National Support
Programme for the Vanguard sites. That programme

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:39.920
will be commissioned collectively by the Vanguards,
and it is a way of setting out the work programme

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:47.710
for us for the year ahead, a way of the Vanguards
holding us to account for what we will do

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:54.899
to support them, and it is also a means for
the Board and the public to be able to see

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:59.189
whether or not we are on track.

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:09.380
Thirdly, there is more in play here than many
of the sites I think appreciate in terms of

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:19.370
our commitment and willingness to examine
barriers to faster progress, and we are strongly

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:27.450
encouraging sites not to limit and to constrain
their ambition. I think this is particularly

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:38.180
true in relation to redesigning workforce
roles and making use of digital technology.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:50.590
Fourthly, NHS England and indeed our myriad
other partners will back the Vanguard sites.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.599
The National New Care Models Board - which
I chair with Adrian Masters at Monitor and

00:02:55.599 --> 00:03:02.519
involves many other partners, NHS clinical
commissioners, NHS providers and voluntary

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:10.450
sector organisations - is 100% behind the
programme, as are all of the arm's-length-body

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:16.550
chief executives who co-authored the Forward
View, and they are committed to supporting

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:25.110
and sponsoring individual Vanguard sites.
We have seen in the last week or so a very

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:33.629
clear public statement of support as well
from the Academy of Medical Royal Colleges,

00:03:33.629 --> 00:03:41.120
expressing the strong intention of the Royal
Colleges to back the Vanguard sites in developing

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.099
new workforce roles.

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:54.580
Fifthly, across the country, we know outside
of the vanguard sites many communities are

00:03:54.580 --> 00:04:01.599
actively progressing their plans for developing
multi-specialty community providers and primary

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:08.260
and acute care systems, as well as better
health in care homes, and we strongly encourage

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:14.310
those sites and give them permission to crack
on with their plans.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:23.440
My final point I wanted to draw out is the
launch this week, jointly between NHS England,

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:34.000
Monitor and the Trust Development Authority,
of our approach to acute care collaboration,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.660
where we are inviting interest from acute
providers to take forward in essence the Dalton

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:51.530
Review recommendations about developing stronger
ties, for example through accountable clinical

00:04:51.530 --> 00:05:01.350
networks, through specialty NHS franchises
such as Moorfields, The Marsden, or whole

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:11.199
NHS organisational chains, the creation of
NHS foundation groups. This work I think is

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:19.570
very important, and provides new strategic
options for our hospital sector in terms of

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:24.220
their future direction in addition to the
option of vertical integration through the

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:26.270
PACS model.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:34.650
Finally, I would add that the programme's
philosophy is about being locally-driven and

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:40.930
nationally supported, and in line with that
philosophy I am delighted that Karen can be

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.620
here today to take us to the Isle of Wight
and run through her plans.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:52.229
Karen Baker: Thanks very much, Ian. Thank
you for that introduction. Thank you for the

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:56.630
opportunity to talk about what is happening
on the Isle of Wight. I will say that I am

00:05:56.630 --> 00:06:01.160
the Chief Executive of the NHS Trust, but
I am very much here on behalf of partners.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.520
We see ourselves as a single leadership team
now, and it could be any one of us doing this

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:05.830
presentation.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:11.669
What I am going to take you through is the
context of the island, where we are now, where

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:17.280
we want to be, how we are going to get there,
then finally how you can help us in order

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.819
to fast-track our plans. Before I start though,
I just wanted to say I very much resonate

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:28.490
with Simon's Chief Exec's update. Part of
my organisation is the acute sector and it

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.100
is under a huge amount of pressure, and I
suppose my message to the Board would be to

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:36.560
keep a really steady hand on the tiller whilst
we are under all of this pressure as we design

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.210
the new models of care, because that is the
thing that really is going to make a massive

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:55.229
difference to the future of the NHS. That
is my personal view on what 
is happening.

00:06:55.229 --> 00:07:00.910
First of all on slide one, the Isle of Wight
has a 140,000 population. We have about 2.5

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:08.130
million visitors come to us all the year round.
We are clearly surrounded by sea. When you

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:12.169
think of the Isle of Wight, you probably think
of Cowes Week and you think of lots of affluence,

00:07:12.169 --> 00:07:17.699
but just to contextualise that, 20% of our
children live in poverty, so we do have some

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:22.669
really huge challenges there around public
health. Our age profile - I think this is

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:27.090
the important thing - is that one in four
of our population is over the age of 65, and

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:32.270
that is a demographic that England will have
in about 20 to 30 years, so we are already

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:38.310
there. With that, more than one in ten are
over 75 years of age, so you can see that

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:42.639
we already have that complexity and frailty
on the Isle of Wight.

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:51.610
Moving on to the next slide, three years ago
the local authority, the CCG and the acute

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:57.400
trust realised that we needed to get together
to define how we needed to work in the future.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:02.680
We had realised we had a challenge of geographical
isolation. We had a growing elderly population

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:08.169
and resources were limited. We had increasing
health and care vacancies, and a pot of money

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:12.819
that was going to be running out and certainly
not getting any bigger. We said we needed

00:08:12.819 --> 00:08:16.660
to fundamentally change the way we worked,
and that was about changing the balance of

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:21.699
power, so changing it away from statutory
organisations to the people, what really matters

00:08:21.699 --> 00:08:26.150
to the people on the Isle of Wight. We needed
to collaborate and cooperate with each other,

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.729
and we needed to really engage with the community.

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:34.459
At that very beginning, a key thing for us
was to say, 'How will we work as leaders across

00:08:34.459 --> 00:08:39.120
systems?' That is a massive challenge, because
you are no longer organisationally centric;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.740
you are working with other people to absolutely
focus on what is right for the population.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:49.310
We developed a set of behaviours as leaders,
and we take ourselves back to those; particularly

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:53.380
in difficult times when things are not going
well, it is quite easy to revert to type,

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:58.200
so we remind each other that we will behave
in a way that progresses the system forward.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.230
We set out our vision. We called it My Life
is a Full Life. We struggled with the title,

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:08.990
but I think that really describes that it
is not just about extending years; it is about

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.560
making sure that people live full and rich
lives for the number of years that they are

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:14.350
here.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:20.070
We started then to engage with the local population,
and you will see on this slide, 'What do people

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:25.120
want?' We used national voices and the I-statements
for that, and we really asked the population,

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.380
'How do you want to modify this?' I think
the first one is the one that they particularly

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:35.610
looked at: 'I will no longer be a patient
or a client; I will be a person.' That is

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.420
a fundamental shift in the way that you are
looking at people. To do that, we developed

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:45.180
a single point of access and we got an Urgent
Care Hub, and in the Urgent Care Hub we have

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:51.040
999, 111, district nurses, social care, allied
health professionals, hospital transport,

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.360
a switchboard, and every time I go to the
Urgent Care Hub, there is another service

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:55.940
in there.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:01.320
You might say, 'So what? What difference will
that make?' An early example was the paramedics

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:06.500
being co-located with the district nurses.
They started to think about sharing skills,

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:11.209
so we now have paramedics who can cauterise
patients, we have district nurses who can

00:10:11.209 --> 00:10:16.170
do advanced wound closures, and that means
that people in the community get just one

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.300
visit, they do not have to have a number of
different professionals coming out to them,

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.910
so they have multi-skilled professionals coming
to see them.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:27.670
The other thing is they share information;
they overhear each other and they listen to

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:32.519
what is going on, so they can help each other
with the kind of care packages that they are

00:10:32.519 --> 00:10:36.880
developing. Also, being in close proximity,
they just talk to each other, so they have

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:42.100
a huge amount of support and they really make
things work for the island population. We

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:46.950
also have integrated locality teams, so we
divided the island into three. They are headed

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:52.680
up by GPs, and they are multi-professional
teams; they have district nurses, social care,

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:59.060
the voluntary sector. We are bringing on nursing
homes and rest homes, and we have successes

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:03.680
in acute care across diabetes and dermatology.
I have to say though that acute care is a

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.240
bit slow to this table, and I take responsibility
for that as the leader of acute care. I think

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:12.730
people look at hospitals and it is very difficult
to imagine what the future looks like, what

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:17.760
outpatient departments look like in the future.
We have been saying we really need to sweep

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.459
away what we currently have and completely
change how we deliver care. That is a massive

00:11:23.459 --> 00:11:25.230
challenge for us.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:31.660
If I move on to slide four, this was the strategic
direction we set. It is called Beyond Boundaries.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:37.170
I have talked about working beyond personal
boundaries and organisational boundaries.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:42.269
We have to work across the Solent, as well,
so we must work with mainland providers, particularly

00:11:42.269 --> 00:11:47.190
around tertiary services, but also secondary
services. We are very conscious that we could

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:52.100
become isolated and vulnerable, so in order
to make sure we do not do that, we work with

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:56.519
partners. You can see some of the partners
there on the map. That is the direction we

00:11:56.519 --> 00:11:58.380
are travelling in.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:05.260
The next slide, what is the current model
at the moment? My Life a Full Life absolutely

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:09.930
overarches everything we do; we see ourselves
as that single leadership team. That is our

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:15.130
brand, and it is a very strong brand on the
island; everybody recognises it. At the moment

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:18.470
we have many services, but they are not all
quite joined-up as they should be. There are

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:22.769
some good examples of where they are, but
there is still duplication.

00:12:22.769 --> 00:12:28.790
If I just use a quick story about a guy called
Bob. He was a 94-year-old man with dementia.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:33.899
He was cared for by his wife at home, and
she suddenly died; she had a heart attack

00:12:33.899 --> 00:12:39.399
and died, so it left him in a very vulnerable
situation. Our Crisis Response Team went in

00:12:39.399 --> 00:12:42.970
there. They had never heard of Bob before,
they did not know what was going on. They

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.540
went in and they had to put him into a residential
home for a number of days whilst they designed

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:52.730
a care package around him that enabled him
to stay at home for the last 18 months of

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:57.320
his life. That care happened, but in addition
to that, a member of the voluntary sector

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:01.250
would go round and take him out to the pub
whenever he wanted. So they really helped

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:03.769
with that care for Bob and what was important
to him.

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:08.230
If I move on to how things should look in
the future, which is the next slide, we would

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:12.930
have known about Bob, so he would not have
had to go into a residential home. The community

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:17.829
would know; we would have shared IT, we would
know what was going on. They would have told

00:13:17.829 --> 00:13:21.170
their story only once; he would have been
on the radar for somebody to say, 'We know

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:25.089
about this family, we realise that there could
be something vulnerable happening there.'

00:13:25.089 --> 00:13:30.420
We would be increasing social capital, as
well, so we would have teams working together,

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:36.160
not just statutory teams, but also voluntary
sector and also carers involved in what we

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:37.010
are doing.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:52.480
If I move you on to slide seven, just in detail
more about the model. The blue on the outside

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:58.279
represents statutory functions. The green
represents generally the voluntary sector,

00:13:58.279 --> 00:14:06.010
and education, and other aspects. The centre
is about social capital. For us, it is about

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:12.550
changing the dimensions of those, so very
much growing the central dimension, growing

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:17.380
the next dimension, and shrinking the statutory
function. You often hear people talk about

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:21.600
moving care to the community is not going
to save us any money; everybody says it is

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:26.399
no cheaper to provide care in the community.
The key for our care model is that we really

00:14:26.399 --> 00:14:31.949
release that social capital, we really work
hard with the voluntary sector, and we get

00:14:31.949 --> 00:14:36.089
them to help us to build services that keep
people independent for longer, so they need

00:14:36.089 --> 00:14:41.440
less statutory-sector intervention in the
future. It will still have to be there - we

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:46.389
will always I think need people to have beds
where they are cared for - but I think that

00:14:46.389 --> 00:14:50.620
is the way that we need to move into the future.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:56.100
Moving on to the next slide on outcomes, outcomes
are important not only for the people we are

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:00.550
caring for, but also for the staff that are
providing those services, and we are working

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:05.500
very closely with the University of Southampton
on measuring this. They are looking at numbers,

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:09.680
experience of people working in the system,
getting it right for everyone, and I think

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:13.180
it is difficult to measure in Bob's story
how right that was for him, to be able to

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:19.899
go out to the pub and carry on his normal
life as it was before. University of Southampton

00:15:19.899 --> 00:15:25.860
are also developing models to see how we can
move care from one sector to another, and

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.850
they are also engaging with health economists
so we can find out what is happening and how

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:34.990
can we really make sure that these are cost-effective,
sustainable services. That is absolutely transferrable

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:39.829
to other systems, and when we are talking
about doing things in a unique environment

00:15:39.829 --> 00:15:43.740
we have to look and say, 'How can that be
translated somewhere else?' I think the model

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:47.800
that Southampton are developing is really
quite exciting.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:55.759
Moving on to the next slide, this is about
our organisational changes. We thought this

00:15:55.759 --> 00:16:01.139
was an ambitious plan, and it maps out over
the coming 12 months what we plan to do, but

00:16:01.139 --> 00:16:04.860
I have to say that the two-day visit from
the New Models of Care team really moved our

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:09.940
thinking on. We thought we were being quite
radical, but having that support and that

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:13.670
push while they were with us really made us
change things. I suppose an example of that

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:17.420
was the commissioners had been talking at
the beginning of the couple of days, 'We need

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:21.180
to do an acute services review.' By the end
of the couple of days, we were all saying,

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.910
'Actually, we need to review all of our services
and see how each and every one of them can

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:30.040
be provided in a different way.' It may sound
small, but that is a huge mind-shift for everybody

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:34.139
in the leadership team, so that is fantastic,
and I think there is something there that

00:16:34.139 --> 00:16:37.300
we need to capture and we need to make sure
as we develop the new models of care that

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:43.660
we keep that energy going and we keep pushing
each other to do things more quickly.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:48.959
Finally - and this I think is the crunch point
for us - what do we need your help with? You

00:16:48.959 --> 00:16:55.519
will see on that slide we have put it into
five categories. I think four of those will

00:16:55.519 --> 00:16:59.220
be achieved through help from the national
networks and from the national team. One of

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:00.899
them is definitely a local issue.

00:17:00.899 --> 00:17:05.490
I will take us through, first of all, one
information. We have achieved quite a lot

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:10.510
in terms of joining up our information. We
have challenges, as everybody else does. I

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:15.669
think the thing for us is that we need help
with information governance. That is national

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:20.730
stuff, so we will do that only once. Everybody
needs that help; how do you share social care

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:26.919
records with GP records, with acute-sector
records? We have a specific broadband issue

00:17:26.919 --> 00:17:31.230
on the island; that is for us to sort out
with our partners, so that is, again, something

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:36.600
that we will do locally. Then we look at system
interoperability, so systems talking to each

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:41.010
other, how do we make that happen? I think
that is a mix of both, so some of that is

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:44.679
locally solved and some of it will be with
national support.

00:17:44.679 --> 00:17:51.770
Looking at the next segment, which is pathway
approach, designing new pathways, I think

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.419
that will be a mix of local and national;
there will be something that the national

00:17:54.419 --> 00:17:59.720
team can help us with. Governance of the system,
so a new system approach which means we do

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:05.010
not have our current organisations. That is
a national issue. Outcome-based commissioning;

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:09.190
I heard Jane saying that magic-wand question.
I really liked that question; I have written,

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:13.669
'My magic wand,' on the back of here. That
is about outcome-based commissioning. That

00:18:13.669 --> 00:18:18.520
has to be a national solution: reduce the
complexity, focus on what is right for the

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:24.510
people, and simplify that for us. Financial
and economic modelling, I would say that is

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.850
a mix of both, so there is something local
there we will be doing, but again, as I was

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:30.940
saying earlier, that is transferrable to national
models.

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:37.570
If I move us on then to workforce, designing
new roles, I think that is national. I think

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:43.000
we certainly work nationally with you. Making
My Life a Full Life a good place to work,

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:46.190
well, clearly, that is down to us. We need
to make it a fantastic place to work on the

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:51.190
island, and I think we are getting there.
How to make the system more locally-based,

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:58.470
I think that will be facilitated by national
help, because I think, again, systems will

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:04.100
struggle with this. How do we make sure we
get across those organisational boundaries?

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:11.640
On organisational form, for me, it is national.
We need to be saying, 'What do those integrated

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:17.400
care organisations look like?' Let us use
the central resource to really work with legal

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:22.270
teams, how should we redesign systems so that
we have a new organisation? I do not think

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:27.059
we should be doing that locally at all, apart
from having conversations about saying we

00:19:27.059 --> 00:19:32.000
really want to do this. The change that has
to happen will clearly happen locally. I think

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:35.289
nationally you can help us with what should
it look like.

00:19:35.289 --> 00:19:39.030
The one I missed out was system leadership
development. I think generally that is going

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:45.419
to be quite a lot of local input. We need
to have the space and the time for local people

00:19:45.419 --> 00:19:50.929
to be able to move these changes forward,
in particular clinical teams. We have talked

00:19:50.929 --> 00:19:56.419
about the fact that recruitment is quite difficult
across the patch; we know that GPs struggle

00:19:56.419 --> 00:20:00.090
to recruit, so there are not lots of people
there to help us with these changes, and we

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:06.070
really need that to happen. I think we do
need national networks, and my view is that

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:11.220
we should have them focused on things. If
I looked, for instance, at perhaps communications

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.309
and public engagement, I think on the island
we have done a great job, and when you look

00:20:14.309 --> 00:20:18.280
at our brand, it is very, very strong. You
talk to people about it; we have spent a lot

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:23.309
of time designing that. Is there a way that
we could have a national network in which

00:20:23.309 --> 00:20:28.419
the Isle of Wight took a really leading role
and helped the other Vanguard sites to say,

00:20:28.419 --> 00:20:32.630
'How have you done that and what can we learn
from you?' There is definitely a way of structuring

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:37.030
that, and I think I would be very interested
in seeing how we can move that on.

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:42.100
In summary, I think there does need to be
real investment locally. I see you smile.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:46.210
It is always going to be a difficult one,
because time costs money, and in order to

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:50.150
get people's engagement we need to make sure
that we put some money into that and we really

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:55.760
value their time. I think we do need to make
sure that we develop the focused national

00:20:55.760 --> 00:21:01.140
networks around all the other enablers. I
think there are lots of things we can be sharing.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:04.360
Take the current Vanguard experts; there will
be people out there who have done much better

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:09.280
on organisational form than us, for instance,
so they could lead a national push on how

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.789
we do that. I think it is very much a mixture.

00:21:12.789 --> 00:21:15.570
Thank you very much for the opportunity to
talk about the island, and I am very happy

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:36.669
now to take any questions and comments from
around the Board.

00:21:36.669 --> 00:21:44.190
Tim Kelsey: These are common themes which
I am sure we will be very happy to support

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:49.789
you develop. There were just two bits that
I was going to ask you about. One was what

00:21:49.789 --> 00:21:54.169
you could loosely describe as customer insight,
modern techniques of ensuring the patient

00:21:54.169 --> 00:22:00.450
voice is in the conversation, not just so
you can design a service, coproduce it, think

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:03.740
closely with the community about how you are
going to develop your models of care, but

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:09.299
also so that they can be evaluated. I just
wondered if you could loosely describe that

00:22:09.299 --> 00:22:13.669
as market research? I think that is something
that is not necessarily home and dry in the

00:22:13.669 --> 00:22:17.080
Health Service, really having the voice in
the room, if I can put it like that. I just

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:23.110
wonder if that is an area that you on reflection
feel can be supported nationally or in other

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:28.200
ways. I would be interested in your views
on how you are getting the patient, the public,

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:32.429
in the design process.

00:22:32.429 --> 00:22:36.500
I went to a Vanguard in Wakefield just a couple
of weeks ago; brilliant, fantastic. I think

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:40.179
when we worked through with them some of the
issues around the great opportunities for

00:22:40.179 --> 00:22:44.900
digital technology to promote channel shift,
to use the jargon, or to help support people

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:51.000
make better decisions, it is really important
that in each step you take you can demonstrate

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.450
value, human as well as efficiency value;
you can actually take the Finance Director

00:22:54.450 --> 00:23:01.280
with you as well as the patient. I wonder
whether you feel you have the right capabilities

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:06.929
to be able to say which order you are going
to do things in based on evidence of their

00:23:06.929 --> 00:23:11.020
contribution to efficiency, their contribution
to quality, and whether there is actually

00:23:11.020 --> 00:23:15.940
in here a requirement possibly for support
around what you could loosely describe as

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:21.330
digital design in the context of evidence
that will make sure that each step amounts

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:27.460
to a measurable, impactful journey, or will
put you on that road. There are two areas

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:27.710
there.

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:31.799
Karen Baker: Great questions. The first one,
definitely we do not have the patient in the

00:23:31.799 --> 00:23:38.419
room. I think it is not where it should be.
I would reflect our local experience. In terms

00:23:38.419 --> 00:23:43.919
of putting a brand on this, My Life a Full
Life, it really has engaged the population.

00:23:43.919 --> 00:23:46.570
What we are all saying is, we walk around
with a t-shirt on with the My Life brand on

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:51.169
there, and also the voluntary sector, and
also the carers, so it is how you get the

00:23:51.169 --> 00:23:55.850
social capital and the people who are doing
lots of that caring to feel as if they are

00:23:55.850 --> 00:24:00.789
part of this whole movement that is trying
to make everybody's life a full life. I think

00:24:00.789 --> 00:24:04.500
there is something around that. There is always
more we can be doing on it.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:09.200
Talking about technology, so how we bring
technology into the room, how we make sure

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:15.720
that those things are cost-effective, I was
at a Clark meeting only this week, and that

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:19.029
is where the University of Southampton were
talking about that modelling, so really looking

00:24:19.029 --> 00:24:24.650
at trying to say, 'So if I did this, it would
be of benefit to the people.' There is that

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:29.549
health economics issue, and they had a health
economist in the room. He kind of said, 'So,

00:24:29.549 --> 00:24:35.890
how is the model coming on?' Well, not really;
a model is useful, but it does not really

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:39.659
predict the future. So I think there is a
huge amount of work we can do on that.

00:24:39.659 --> 00:24:43.549
The digital technology, I think again there
is more we need to do, because I think when

00:24:43.549 --> 00:24:47.809
we first started looking at digital technology,
so people monitoring themselves in their own

00:24:47.809 --> 00:24:51.799
homes, when that first came out, the Medical
Director said to me, 'That is all well and

00:24:51.799 --> 00:24:55.510
good; just phone them up and ask them how
they feel. You will get the same sort of response.'

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:59.260
However, I have to say, he has shifted in
his thinking now. So I think as technology

00:24:59.260 --> 00:25:04.490
advances, there definitely will be real benefits
from that, and I think you are right: we have

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:08.120
to scrutinise it and say, 'Will this really
make a difference or not?' We are not there

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:11.100
yet, and I think some national help on that
would be really useful.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:18.070
Margaret Casely-Hayford: First of all I wanted
to say thank you, because that was a really

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:24.169
superb exposition, really clear and enormously
helpful. Thank you very much indeed. Then

00:25:24.169 --> 00:25:30.090
I have a couple of questions, or perhaps it
may be two or three questions, but you can

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:31.779
unpack it as you like.

00:25:31.779 --> 00:25:37.090
The first one is that you talked about the
way in which the partners are all involved

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:41.100
together and all equal, and in fact you actually
led by saying that anyone could have come

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:49.700
in and given the exposition. That is great,
but to the extent that you need to drive implementation,

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:57.419
do you feel that there should be one project
leader, and do you feel that not having that

00:25:57.419 --> 00:26:02.700
could slow or in any way hamper progress?
If you do feel that there should be one project

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:10.110
leader, how would that impact on the other
partners and how would that lead emerge?

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:15.600
The next question is really to do with the
fact that the Isle of Wight is a very clearly

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:26.950
identifiable place, and in talking about scalability
of Vanguards, your model looks great. How

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:38.789
do you think that the model could be easily
scaled up, impacting in other geographical

00:26:38.789 --> 00:26:43.380
locations that are not so obviously a place?
I do not deny that you did talk about working

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:49.549
across boundaries, and not least across geography,
with the Solent not really being a divide,

00:26:49.549 --> 00:26:56.250
and that is helpful, but if you could just
give a view on that, that would be helpful.

00:26:56.250 --> 00:27:05.279
The next question is about Bob, and it sort
of attaches to Tim's point about communication

00:27:05.279 --> 00:27:10.460
and hearing the patient voice, because if
we are talking about social capital, forgive

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:17.159
me for putting it this way, but I think perhaps
if we listened to the carers at the right

00:27:17.159 --> 00:27:24.580
point we could get better social capital from
them, because if Mrs Bob were still alive

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:31.309
that would probably mean that he would still
be very much in the situation he was before

00:27:31.309 --> 00:27:38.690
and being looked after by her, which is probably
what both of them would want. One has to think

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:44.380
about what longevity she might have had if
she had had a voice earlier on in the process

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:51.140
and still been involved. So whether in fact
we are listening temporally at the right point

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:54.090
to the patients is my final question.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:59.390
Karen Baker: Thank you very much for those
questions. Certainly I made a point about

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:04.789
the partners, and I think what you are saying
is you need a leader to make things happen.

00:28:04.789 --> 00:28:09.679
John Rivers, the Chair of the Clinical Commissioning
Group, is our leader. We are all completely

00:28:09.679 --> 00:28:12.960
comfortable with that; in fact, somebody asked
me the other day, 'How does John feel about

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:16.750
you going to NHS England?' I said, 'He is
not going to be worried at all.' We speak

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:20.880
with the same voice. I think if we get into
a sticky situation, then certainly we will

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:21.850
look to John.

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:26.270
I think it is really important to have team
leadership. Each one of us has a different

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:30.470
strength, and we need to bring those strengths
to the table. So I agree that I think we need

00:28:30.470 --> 00:28:35.649
one person, but I do think we need to work
out how we are going in the same direction

00:28:35.649 --> 00:28:41.080
as a team, as well as having somebody who
arbitrates, I think you are saying, when you

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.500
are all disagreeing. I think that is right.

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:47.880
On scalability, the way I think about the
island is as a bit of a petri dish. When you

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:51.580
are trying to grow something in a laboratory,
you have a look at what you are growing, and

00:28:51.580 --> 00:28:57.279
then what you can do is take elements of what
you have and transfer them to other areas.

00:28:57.279 --> 00:29:00.100
What I have tried to do in the presentation
is talk about where I see those elements,

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:04.700
and I think the measurement piece is very
much something that would be transferable,

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:08.990
the modelling piece; just because it is modelled
on the island does not mean that it is not

00:29:08.990 --> 00:29:14.409
applicable to other areas. The changes in
the workforce that you have been talking about

00:29:14.409 --> 00:29:18.899
as a Board this morning, those things are
absolutely transferrable. Lots of the elements

00:29:18.899 --> 00:29:22.529
are, and as with any bit of the NHS, some
of it is and some of it is not; whether it

00:29:22.529 --> 00:29:27.049
is the island or whether it is Harrogate,
there will be elements that are and are not.

00:29:27.049 --> 00:29:31.980
I think the trick for us though is, as an
NHS, really, really engaging and learning

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:35.320
from each other. We are great at developing
new things, and then we say, 'Do you know,

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:40.130
it is just different here.' I do not buy that.
I think sometimes we use that difference as

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:43.309
an obstacle not to implement the right things.

00:29:43.309 --> 00:29:48.720
Finally, your question around Bob: do we hear
the carer's voice soon enough? I do not think

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:53.380
we do. I think that is a massive challenge,
because that is where, three years ago, we

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:57.320
started to say shifting the balance of power.
You have talked about personal care budgets;

00:29:57.320 --> 00:30:01.520
I think that is fantastic. You talked about
learning disabilities. We had that on the

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:08.190
island and we saw the whole service transformed
by staff moving into a joint venture and setting

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:13.140
their own learning disabilities service up
purely because people had their personal budgets.

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.960
What they have done now is that they have
really changed those services to meet the

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:20.779
needs of those individuals. I think we do
not listen soon enough, and I think we do

00:30:20.779 --> 00:30:24.720
need to listen more. I think we have lots
of elements here on the island where we can

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:30.590
start listening to people sooner in the journey.
Thank you.

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:35.919
John Burn: Malcolm, as you know, I had the
benefit of spending a day with Karen, John

00:30:35.919 --> 00:30:40.890
and the team, and I think just a couple of
thoughts, really. I think one of the things

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:48.659
that we need to as a Board continue to think
upon, the first thing I would say is at the

00:30:48.659 --> 00:30:54.370
heart of this is an aligned leadership team
across multiple organisational forms. Actually,

00:30:54.370 --> 00:31:00.010
the leadership team supplants the form, but
we have to find the roots to make the mechanics

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:07.559
support that, because they get in the way.
Alignment of leadership, which has taken time,

00:31:07.559 --> 00:31:11.070
is now proving the value of that investment
in time.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:19.159
I think the second thing is I would not underestimate
the power of the branding and the engagement

00:31:19.159 --> 00:31:24.000
that has come out of that, because actually
what was striking to me and what I learned

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:29.659
massively from that day was actually the power
of the voluntary sector and the community

00:31:29.659 --> 00:31:37.519
in engaging and making the solutions work.
That comes because of an engagement and, to

00:31:37.519 --> 00:31:42.640
a degree, a marketing model and an engagement
model and a branding model and a communication

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:48.240
model that means that people feel they can
become part of the solution rather than having

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:53.899
it done unto them. I think as we think about
how these various models in all their different

00:31:53.899 --> 00:32:01.890
shapes and forms start to grow, then actually
we need I think to help and support a number

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:07.899
of these Vanguards to think about how they
market and brand and engage their own workforce,

00:32:07.899 --> 00:32:13.419
but also the wider community in particular
the voluntary sector. I thought that was a

00:32:13.419 --> 00:32:16.860
tactical issue when I was there; it actually
strikes me as a strategic issue, because I

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:21.980
think it brings a resource and capability
that helps make this work.

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:26.990
I think the third observation I would make
is I came away with two thoughts, one of which

00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:35.120
is, given that the Isle of Wight is to some
extent a laboratory of how parts of England,

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:40.960
Wales, Scotland, Northern Ireland etc., will
look in years to come, actually the sustainability

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:47.399
of the care-home sector is a strategic issue
which is not in our remit, but is an area

00:32:47.399 --> 00:32:52.250
where we are hugely dependent and where I
think we need to give some thoughts.

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:58.690
The final thing is we are going to have to
be brave collectively about workforce redesign,

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:02.669
because the other thing that came out loud
and clear is that the team there are looking

00:33:02.669 --> 00:33:09.120
to make some very fundamental changes to roles
right across, particularly in the community,

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:13.740
and we are going to have to be quite brave
collectively as a system leadership and as

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:19.730
national bodies and all the various other
things that you worry about, Bruce. That is

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:22.260
going to be an essential part of making this
work as well.

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:28.120
So I think engagement, branding, alignment
of leadership, sustainability of the care-home

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:32.990
sector, and the workforce redesign, but there
is a lot we can learn. There is a lot we can

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:38.980
learn from something that was not a Vanguard,
it was started locally, and now our job is

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:39.919
to help support it.

00:33:39.919 --> 00:33:45.159
Karen Baker: Thanks, John. Could I just respond
to one of those elements, and that is about

00:33:45.159 --> 00:33:50.200
the care-home sector. We are looking on the
island at beds across the island, so what

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:54.460
we are saying now is it is not just about
acute beds; I think some of the pressure certainly

00:33:54.460 --> 00:34:00.100
across the acute sector is the care-home sector,
so we very much have those people around the

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:05.190
table. I think it is important for us to think
about innovative ways of how we help those

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:11.419
people to staff the care sector, because certainly
they struggle with double-handed care packages,

00:34:11.419 --> 00:34:15.799
nursing home recruitment, and if there is
some way that we can all get together on that

00:34:15.799 --> 00:34:19.419
and say, 'How do we help that to succeed?'
I think it is very, very important. It is

00:34:19.420 --> 00:34:20.620
a great point.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:29.140
Karen Wheeler: Could I ask a couple of questions?
I did not hear you mention a lot about capability,

00:34:29.140 --> 00:34:34.540
and I was wondering whether you felt that
you had access to the capability that you

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:41.099
need for making the sort of systemic changes
that you are interested in making, obviously.

00:34:41.099 --> 00:34:49.179
The second relates to the leadership aspect,
which you touched on, but I am really keen

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:56.970
to understand a bit more about what is the
leadership ask, if you like, what are the

00:34:56.970 --> 00:35:02.700
issues and what are the requirements that
you are looking for? Is it in terms of upskilling

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:11.790
or accessing leadership programmes for people
you already have, or new leadership? What

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:12.190
is that?

00:35:12.190 --> 00:35:16.690
Karen Baker: Thank you for that. Capability,
again, that question is always a tricky one

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:23.990
to answer, because everywhere has an element
of capability and elements where we need additional

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:27.750
capability, and again that matches in with
capacity. So when people ask me a question

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:31.930
that says, 'Is it capability or capacity?'
that is quite a tough one to answer; if you

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:38.310
had more time, you might be more capable.
I think we would very much welcome some additional

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:42.670
support in the capability round, and that
is looking to the national team to say, 'What

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:45.329
is it we can cross-fertilise and help with?'

00:35:45.329 --> 00:35:49.589
In terms of the leadership, the key thing
about the leadership that we have done already

00:35:49.589 --> 00:35:54.770
is to have those behaviours. We need vision,
values and behaviours that are aligned across

00:35:54.770 --> 00:36:00.470
the whole system, so you no longer care whether
somebody works for the local authority or

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:03.890
whether they work for the Trust or the voluntary
sector.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:07.609
I think picking up David's point on branding,
it is so important that it is not just the

00:36:07.609 --> 00:36:11.530
brand and the T-shirt we are wearing, but
it is the way we behave towards the people

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:16.550
that we are providing services to. That is
a key point.

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:21.180
System leadership is crucial because people
do just go back into their silos. When times

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:26.420
get tough, people go back into their role.
I was actually talking to somebody from the

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:30.640
Trust Development Authority, and we were unpicking
this slightly and saying, 'Where do you go?'

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:35.310
She said, 'Well, when times get tough, you
revert into your silo,' so you have to get

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:40.760
rid of the silos. Some of the things that
I have heard the Board discussing today almost

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:44.900
start to reinforce the silos, because it is
a system we are working on. We have to get

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.690
our heads around the fact that these things
are getting in the way of providing the right

00:36:48.690 --> 00:37:03.010
care 
for the people in the country.

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:13.720
Ciaran Devane: That was going to be my point.
I think this is beginning to define what our

00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:18.740
role, going forward, should be. It is about
outcomes-based commissioning, it is about

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:23.670
workforce roles, new ones that have not been
invented yet. It is interesting what you are

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:27.140
not asking for: you are not asking for more
performance management, you are not asking

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:37.530
for more forms to fill in. Also, if you go
across what you are asking for, there is a

00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:43.020
lot in it about expertise; 'Can I access information
governance expertise? Can I access restructuring

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:48.329
expertise? Can I access change-management
expertise?' I think there is something we

00:37:48.329 --> 00:37:54.290
need to think about there around saying how
we create the environment, as you were saying,

00:37:54.290 --> 00:37:59.339
for you to share with the other Vanguards,
with the integration pioneers, with general

00:37:59.339 --> 00:38:04.400
enthusiasts, to share and learn from each
other, but how we also play in some of that

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:09.210
deep expertise to say, 'This is what I need
now. I will not need it in three months' time;

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:12.589
I need it today, and in three months' time
it can go off and do something else that is

00:38:12.589 --> 00:38:18.390
useful.' For me, there is something quite
profound in this about the role of the commissioner

00:38:18.390 --> 00:38:21.150
in this to do things in a completely different
way.

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:25.210
Karen Baker: I think that is a very valid
point. You talked about performance management.

00:38:25.210 --> 00:38:29.940
It depends what it is, because actually focusing
in on the fact that we had NHS England coming

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:35.349
for two days gave us a huge amount of energy
and push. So there is something about how

00:38:35.349 --> 00:38:39.280
does the national body say, 'Our expectation
is that you will make these changes.' I think

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:42.490
there is a lot of enthusiasm on the ground,
and there are a lot of fires burning, it is

00:38:42.490 --> 00:38:46.470
just that I think national bodies can fan
those flames to make sure they do not go out:

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:49.810
'We are still interested in what it is you
are doing and we want to give you this support,

00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:53.200
but we also want to see what you are doing
with the support we are giving you.' It is

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:56.180
setting an expectation, I suppose, rather
than performance management.

00:38:56.180 --> 00:39:02.530
Malcolm Grant: Can I just ask, you referred
to the island pound, which I think is a very

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:06.570
powerful concept, and I can see the island
pound in two ways, I suppose. The first is

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:13.490
the obvious way, which is that you pool the
resource so that nobody needs to worry what

00:39:13.490 --> 00:39:17.880
is the source of the funding to do what is
best for patients. I suppose the second aspect

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:23.540
is how do you extract maximum value for money
from the island pound? How do you manage to

00:39:23.540 --> 00:39:30.369
take a fixed resource and spread it more effectively
in providing care and support for the patient

00:39:30.369 --> 00:39:31.160
population?

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:36.050
Karen Baker: Yes. There are other systems,
I think particularly in Spain, where they

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:40.420
look at how they provide capitated budgets,
and then how they actually drive value out

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:44.700
of the system. Certainly there is a model
we can be looking at there. I have also been

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:50.510
talking to Optum, the American company, and
they are looking at how to drive up quality

00:39:50.510 --> 00:39:54.510
and drive down cost, so I think there are
some international models we could be looking

00:39:54.510 --> 00:39:58.710
at that say, 'How do we make sure that we
do not just take the money that is coming

00:39:58.710 --> 00:40:03.180
in and not spend it in the best way? How do
we make sure that we do spend it in the best

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:07.450
way?' Again, I would like some help there
from national expertise and international

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:12.069
expertise to say how we should design that
island pound system so it is simple and it

00:40:12.069 --> 00:40:12.930
drives the right behaviours.

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:26.150
Ed Smith: Firstly, I am massively encouraged
by your talk with respect to behaviours, reducing

00:40:26.150 --> 00:40:31.770
silos, porous boundaries and the role of leadership,
and I note in your sort of Vanguard support

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:38.050
required, there is actually rather a lot there
about leadership and workforce, so I strongly

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:45.450
encourage it. If you were to be asked as a
result today to go and be a consultant or

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:51.119
an advisor to another health economy, and
you were asked the question, 'How can we do

00:40:51.119 --> 00:40:56.390
what you have done but do it in a third of
the time?' what would be some of the things

00:40:56.390 --> 00:40:56.869
that you would say?

00:40:56.869 --> 00:41:02.210
Karen Baker: Well, the first thing I would
say is take away organisational boundaries.

00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:07.900
What I have not alluded to, and you may know
this already, is that the NHS Trust is already

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:13.410
ambulance, community, mental health and acute
services in one trust, and having worked in

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:18.160
mainland organisations that have those silos
between them, I cannot tell you how quickly

00:41:18.160 --> 00:41:22.750
I can get things done on the Isle of Wight
just in that simple part of the system as

00:41:22.750 --> 00:41:27.869
opposed to perhaps sitting in Southampton
trying to negotiate with South Central Ambulance

00:41:27.869 --> 00:41:32.609
Service. It is not that they are not great
organisations on their own, it is just their

00:41:32.609 --> 00:41:36.960
importance; they are kind of thinking, 'Well,
I do not really need to do that. My driver

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:38.790
is to do this.'

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:43.319
Start off by saying you would set up your
new health and social care system exactly

00:41:43.319 --> 00:41:48.980
as it is going to on the island. Take away
the boundaries; put people under the single

00:41:48.980 --> 00:41:53.430
banner of whatever it is, and then start to
get them working. I think again it is about

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:57.220
the leadership development of those teams
so that they do develop that in the right

00:41:57.220 --> 00:42:03.050
way. You would never do the NHS like we did
it in 1948, and the Five Year Forward View

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:08.380
for me is absolutely fundamental in changing
this; it is the most exciting bit of legislation

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:12.520
I have ever seen come out, and I have been
in the NHS for 35 years now. So this is a

00:42:12.520 --> 00:42:15.760
very different time, and that is what I would
do if I were going out somewhere else.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:18.960
Malcolm Grant: What is truly exciting is that
it is not even legislation.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.329
Karen Baker: Sorry. It should be.

00:42:21.329 --> 00:42:27.250
Malcolm Grant: We are moving away from the
legislative model to one which is truly visionary.

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:28.200
Karen Baker: You are right.

00:42:28.200 --> 00:42:34.310
Malcolm Grant: Karen, thank you so much for
that. This is early stage; this is truly still

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:38.010
early stage, and we have the message that
Ian is just going to give us about what we

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:41.310
need to be doing, but the whole point about
them is that it is irreversible. This is not

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:46.560
just a pilot flash in the pan; this is a fundamental
change in the operating model.

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:52.910
Ian, with that build-up, may we come back
to you to reflect on what we have heard from

00:42:52.910 --> 00:42:55.650
Karen and the next steps for NHS England?

00:42:55.650 --> 00:43:07.140
Ian Dodge: I think the challenge to us and
our national partners is to continue to engage

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:16.400
with the Vanguard sites and to channel what
the collective requests are to sift between

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:22.910
what is purely local and the things that can
then be done in common, and to make sure we

00:43:22.910 --> 00:43:30.730
can articulate the right programmes of support
to deliver what you need. That goes back,

00:43:30.730 --> 00:43:40.079
Chair, to the July National Support Programme.
It also goes back to how we can support the

00:43:40.079 --> 00:43:47.410
different cohorts to work together as collectives.
You touched on a number of elements where

00:43:47.410 --> 00:43:53.250
you said, rather than coming up with a unique
Isle of Wight solution, there would be merit

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:59.339
in looking at what the other eight PACS sites
for example are taking forward, and developing

00:43:59.339 --> 00:44:05.180
a single common solution. At the heart of
the replicability question that you posed,

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:11.020
Margaret, is how in six months' time, as a
result of some of the support that we are

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:16.490
developing for groups of people, we then have
a number of different places that are choosing

00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:23.260
to do the same thing, so that if we were then
going to articulate a framework - for example,

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:31.099
for what a PACS is or an NCP is - we would
not be doing this in a traditional kind of

00:44:31.099 --> 00:44:36.990
model of design it in the centre and then
go and ask some people to implement. What

00:44:36.990 --> 00:44:43.440
we would have done instead is co-design what
this thing is in a number of different sites,

00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:48.760
and we would be learning from the experience
of the integration pioneers in that, rather

00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:54.970
than producing the framework with local case
studies of the unique things that different

00:44:54.970 --> 00:45:00.790
areas have done, we would be pointing to what
groups of people are doing in common that

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:05.780
gets beyond just the heroic local leadership
and the relationships.

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:12.150
I think on many of the questions you have
posed about how we simplify the system, if

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:19.500
the NHS is a complex, adaptive system, it
is arguably far too complex and insufficiently

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:24.790
adaptive, and we have a massive opportunity
I think to help with the simplification of

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:32.190
it, focused around people, focused around
breaking down those boundaries between the

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:38.990
historical settings of primary care, community
services, hospital bed services, social care

00:45:38.990 --> 00:45:46.710
services and social care beds. This would
free you up to give you a potentially bundled

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:52.540
pot of cash and support you to develop the
right organisational forms that are not the

00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:59.140
ends in themselves, but are some of the enabling
wrappers for you then to be able to progress

00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:06.270
the rethinking of technology, of workforce
roles, of patient and community engagement,

00:46:06.270 --> 00:46:08.000
because those are the things that are really
going to drive change.

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:14.359
Malcolm Grant: I am not going to allow this
to run on for too much longer, because we

00:46:14.359 --> 00:46:20.599
are getting far too excited about it and there
was so much learning from Karen to absorb,

00:46:20.599 --> 00:46:29.940
and actually the challenges for us from this
are not trivial, they are really very serious.

00:46:29.940 --> 00:46:37.050
David Roberts: From the perspective of this
Board, the Commissioning Committee is overseeing

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:43.390
and governing the aggregation of what the
requirements are and how we then set about

00:46:43.390 --> 00:46:47.510
to fix them, and what are the implications
for NHS England within that. Undoubtedly,

00:46:47.510 --> 00:46:52.540
we will bring that back to the full Board
at some stage.

00:46:52.540 --> 00:47:02.530
Margaret Casely-Hayford: What can we do to
help in terms of collection of material evidence,

00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:16.500
data, about demographics, locale and so on,
that would make it easier to map across, for

00:47:16.500 --> 00:47:25.599
the purposes of replicability? In other words,
I suppose it is the question of how you cut

00:47:25.599 --> 00:47:33.220
down the time again for replicability, because
we have already identified issues, we have

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:40.510
identified similarities. As the national body,
we have the information, we have the evidence.

00:47:40.510 --> 00:47:46.530
What do you see - I think it is a question
for both you and Ian - as the essence of the

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:49.920
evidence that we could retain to help with
that?

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:53.680
Karen Baker: Can I just talk about the Vanguards?
Even meeting other Vanguard sites, we are

00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:58.559
starting to learn about what is similar about
our demographics. So it has been very, very

00:47:58.559 --> 00:48:02.530
powerful getting together with, for instance,
Harrogate, who also have challenges around

00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:07.829
an elderly, frail population. That is a start,
and moving it into the whole national picture,

00:48:07.829 --> 00:48:12.150
I think I will hand over to Ian to talk about
how we might do that.

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:18.059
Ian Dodge: An integral part of the support
programme will be the work on operational

00:48:18.059 --> 00:48:26.150
research and evaluation supported by the new
unit we have established, which will be looking

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:35.170
to develop a set of common metrics and ways
of understanding the active ingredients of

00:48:35.170 --> 00:48:42.180
change and the causation, the logic models
and the causation, of what people are doing

00:48:42.180 --> 00:48:47.990
in local sites that is then driving those
outcomes. Our challenge and opportunity here

00:48:47.990 --> 00:48:54.480
is to do that very actively and intensively
with all the local sites, and make sure that

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:58.180
that happens faster than is traditionally
the case.

00:48:58.180 --> 00:49:02.440
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Karen, so much for
coming and talking to us this morning. I think

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:07.290
there are many messages that we will take
away, but one that resonates I think is leadership,

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:11.720
and the leadership that you have demonstrated,
but it is a team model of leadership and the

00:49:11.720 --> 00:49:18.630
behaviours that that demonstrates, and the
issue for us is, as we develop working with

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:23.589
further Vanguards across the country, how
we encourage, facilitate and enable local

00:49:23.589 --> 00:49:30.900
leadership to emerge and to allow, as I have
heard in visiting Vanguards, a group of people

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:36.520
to do what they have always wanted to do but
have been prevented by the structures that

00:49:36.520 --> 00:49:40.849
exist within the system. Thank you very much
for joining us this morning, and thank you

00:49:40.849 --> 00:49:42.260
Ian for the paper.

